description: Baseline project defaults for any repo using Kio — scope, code hygiene, git/secrets, DB/prod safety, evidence before guesses
alwaysApply: true
---

# Project defaults

- Change only what the task needs. Do not refactor broadly unless asked.
- Match existing patterns, naming, and file layout; do not invent a parallel architecture.
- Do not edit unrelated files or do drive-by refactors.
- Prefer existing libraries; do not add a dependency if the repo already covers the need.
- Do not change public APIs or schemas without stating the impact.
- Do not hardcode secrets or log credentials/PII.
- Do not swallow errors with empty catch blocks.
- Do not commit, push, amend, force-push, or change git config unless the user explicitly asks.
- Never commit secrets (`.env`, credentials, tokens, private keys).
- Read real files in the repo before concluding how something works. Do not invent APIs, paths, or owners.
- If a decision is blocked by missing info, ask one short question instead of guessing.
- Prefer the smallest clear fix over a large redesign.
- Do not run destructive DB operations (DROP, TRUNCATE, delete-all, DB reset, irreversible migrate) unless the user explicitly asks.
- Do not touch production or shared databases without explicit confirmation.
- Prefer existing migration workflows; do not invent or rewrite migration history.
- Do not deploy, release, or apply infra to production/shared environments unless the user explicitly asks.
- Do not use production credentials, kube contexts, or connection strings unless the user explicitly confirms that target.
- Do not flush caches, purge queues, or bulk-delete object storage unless the user explicitly asks.
- Do not publish packages/images or trigger user-facing side effects (email, SMS, payments, key rotation) against real systems unless asked.
